#!RuleTest
#test PMDA-SD0061 expect=violation domain=AE
#note "Mirrors rulespec PMDA-SD0061.yaml (the rule's only prior guard; PMDA twin of FDA-SD0061). The Define-XML sidecar declares FOUR datasets (AE, VS, ZZ, YY) but the study submits only AE and VS -> $missing_datasets = [ZZ, YY] and the rule fires once, on the primary dataset's bucket (Sensitivity Study). Pinning the projected $missing_datasets list is what makes this discriminating: leaking the Define's key set into $study_datasets (the D3 inventory hazard the spec guards) un-fires the rule, and a define_dataset_names() that returned [] would change the list and red the pin. The pinned order is the engine's raw projection - Define declaration order minus the study set (the rulespec pins the same set canonical-sorted as [YY, ZZ]; the .cdt harness does not sort)."
#library standard=sdtmig version=3-4
#define-xml PMDA-SD0061-define.xml
#expectViolationCount 1
#expectViolationAt "$missing_datasets"="[ZZ, YY]"
dataset AE label="Adverse Events"
col STUDYID type=Char label="Study Identifier"
col DOMAIN  type=Char label="Domain Abbreviation"
col USUBJID type=Char label="Unique Subject Identifier"
col AESEQ   type=Num  label="Sequence Number"
col AETERM  type=Char label="Reported Term for the Adverse Event"
---
S1 | AE | 001 | 1 | HEADACHE
---

dataset VS label="Vital Signs"
col STUDYID  type=Char label="Study Identifier"
col DOMAIN   type=Char label="Domain Abbreviation"
col USUBJID  type=Char label="Unique Subject Identifier"
col VSSEQ    type=Num  label="Sequence Number"
col VSTESTCD type=Char label="Vital Signs Test Short Name"
---
S1 | VS | 001 | 1 | SYSBP
